## Failures and how to do differently

- Symptom: the full matrix dies in `globalSetup` before tests start. Cause: required auth-state files are missing or stale for one or more products. Fix: confirm `.env`, `.auth`, and product-specific local services first; the Nucleum runner could auto-refresh its own auth once the credentials and services were healthy. [Task 1]
- Symptom: a product-specific auth-save command fails even though another product's auth refresh works. Cause: local host routing differs by product; here `account-insouth-local.memotron.app` did not resolve even though Nucleum endpoints were healthy. Fix: re-check product-specific DNS/routing before assuming auth-save flows are portable across products. [Task 1]
- Symptom: many tail failures look like generic flake after setup succeeds. Cause: they cluster into a few concrete UI families like PDF overlay interception, missing settings controls, or stale seeded-state expectations. Fix: group failures by repeated assertion pattern before deciding whether the suite is still blocked by environment. [Task 1]

# Task Group: authfn/core organization and source provenance in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus
scope: Use for quick architecture opinions about `@authfn/core` from the Nucleus account-service integration, especially when the user asks whether server/runtime concerns and OAuth/provider logic are organized sensibly and whether the answer was grounded in actual repo source. Boundaries: this is source-grounded judgment, not a full package audit; re-check current package layout and symlink targets before reusing exact structure claims.
applies_to: cwd=/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/nucleus with linked package source in /Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/superfunctions-dev/authfn/core; reuse_rule=safe for similar architecture-opinion or provenance questions in this checkout, but keep claims scoped to the inspected files rather than the entire package tree.

